# Port & adapter

| Piece       | Owner                                         | Role                                      |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| **Port**    | consumer (`auth/port/user.port.ts`)           | type only; consumer-owned contract        |
| **Adapter** | provider (`identity/adapter/user.adapter.ts`) | class; one-line delegate to own use-case  |

Wired in [container](./container.mdc): `new UserAdapter(userRepository)`.

## Return types

- A port method is the **provider's** model and returns that shared DTO (`UserModelType`). The consumer loads its own aggregate.
- Consumer-only shapes (e.g. `UserCredentials` on the port) stay on the port — no extra validation type if it is just an intersection.
- No entity pass-through (`independent-modules`: consumer must not import `*.entity.ts`).
- No provider `PersistedType` + `toPersistence` on the port boundary — mapping belongs in the provider application layer.

## Do

- Use-case depends on the port type only
- Adapter method: `return await this.createUser.execute(data)`
- DB-bound adapter: `withTx(tx)` returns a new adapter on `repository.withTx(tx)`. The port declares `withTx` when a use-case calls it inside a transaction
- Put factory → save → `Mapper.toDto` in provider **use-cases** (or a domain **service** when it fits [service](./service.mdc))

## Don't

- Consumer importing provider repo / use-case / entity / adapter
- Port importing adapter
- A port method whose result is another module's list
- Logic in the adapter (add a use-case instead)

```ts
// auth/port/user.port.ts
export type UserPort = {
  create: (input: CreateUserData) => Promise<UserModelType>;
  findByEmail: (email: string) => Promise<UserModelType | null>;
};

// identity/adapter/user.adapter.ts
async create(data: CreateUserData): Promise<UserModelType> {
  return await this.createUser.execute(data);
}
```
